    {
      "id": 17,
      "kind": "mother_usage",
      "payload": "{\"model_calls\":1,\"phase\":\"synthesis\",\"plan\":{\"A\":\"Research serial output batching and formatting. Use the measured 64 KiB reusable buffer with contiguous `fwrite` as the leading reference, then test one output-path change at a time (for example, reducing per-character append overhead). Preserve the exact `[ `, character-and-space, `]\\\\n` format and empty-input behavior. Keep permutation traversal fixed so measurements isolate output changes.\",\"B\":\"Research the in-place lexicographic successor independently of output formatting. Compare the current generic iterator helpers with indexed or standard-library successor implementations, keeping the formatter and buffering fixed for fair timing. Preserve distinct-permutation behavior and lexicographic order for repeated characters; check edge cases such as empty, one-character, and all-identical inputs. Do not attribute the fastest A candidate's gain to traversal alone: its output and traversal changes were bundled.\",\"C\":\"Explore parallel generation only as a cautious, separate experiment. The measured prefix-queue implementation took 0.13986 s, slower even than the 0.13167 s baseline, while the fastest serial candidate took 0.00662 s. Do not repeat per-permutation string allocation and fine-grained queue synchronization unchanged. If testing a coarser bounded-buffer design, preserve lexicographic output by ordered prefix ranges, avoid unbounded memory, retain a correct serial fallback, and reject it unless complete-run measurements show a benefit.\",\"G\":\"Integrate and measure complete candidates rather than assuming changes combine additively. The fastest measured candidate is A `b3a50e4d19b747ed` at 0.00662 s; use it as a comparison point, not an assumed winner for every integrated build. The B variants measured 0.00731 and 0.00737 s, alternate A 0.00742 s, C line buffering 0.00840 s, and C parallel 0.13986 s. Validate exact output and correctness on the available cases for each integrated artifact, then compare repeated end-to-end timings. Merge overlapping changes selectively; keep the serial candidate unless an integrated parallel variant proves faster and correct.\",\"analysis\":\"The task enumerates distinct permutations in lexicographic order and prints every result, so both successor work and output volume matter. Baseline mean was 0.13167 s; the best reported mean was 0.00662 s (about 19.9× faster), from a bundled change using indexed succession and a 64 KiB `fwrite` buffer. Other measured changes centered on per-line output formatting and were slower than that candidate. Because the measurements combine or alter multiple aspects, they do not establish the isolated benefit of any one transformation. The four-core target makes parallel work possible, but the observed queue-based attempt regressed substantially; integration must be measured, not inferred.\"},\"request_sha256\":\"d5bbce62e20ba0fc8b0adec2e99223307782254861bc669c9dea2d3fe2df2910\",\"response\":{\"data\":{\"A\":\"Research serial output batching and formatting. Use the measured 64 KiB reusable buffer with contiguous `fwrite` as the leading reference, then test one output-path change at a time (for example, reducing per-character append overhead). Preserve the exact `[ `, character-and-space, `]\\\\n` format and empty-input behavior. Keep permutation traversal fixed so measurements isolate output changes.\",\"B\":\"Research the in-place lexicographic successor independently of output formatting. Compare the current generic iterator helpers with indexed or standard-library successor implementations, keeping the formatter and buffering fixed for fair timing. Preserve distinct-permutation behavior and lexicographic order for repeated characters; check edge cases such as empty, one-character, and all-identical inputs. Do not attribute the fastest A candidate's gain to traversal alone: its output and traversal changes were bundled.\",\"C\":\"Explore parallel generation only as a cautious, separate experiment. The measured prefix-queue implementation took 0.13986 s, slower even than the 0.13167 s baseline, while the fastest serial candidate took 0.00662 s. Do not repeat per-permutation string allocation and fine-grained queue synchronization unchanged. If testing a coarser bounded-buffer design, preserve lexicographic output by ordered prefix ranges, avoid unbounded memory, retain a correct serial fallback, and reject it unless complete-run measurements show a benefit.\",\"G\":\"Integrate and measure complete candidates rather than assuming changes combine additively. The fastest measured candidate is A `b3a50e4d19b747ed` at 0.00662 s; use it as a comparison point, not an assumed winner for every integrated build. The B variants measured 0.00731 and 0.00737 s, alternate A 0.00742 s, C line buffering 0.00840 s, and C parallel 0.13986 s. Validate exact output and correctness on the available cases for each integrated artifact, then compare repeated end-to-end timings. Merge overlapping changes selectively; keep the serial candidate unless an integrated parallel variant proves faster and correct.\",\"analysis\":\"The task enumerates distinct permutations in lexicographic order and prints every result, so both successor work and output volume matter. Baseline mean was 0.13167 s; the best reported mean was 0.00662 s (about 19.9× faster), from a bundled change using indexed succession and a 64 KiB `fwrite` buffer. Other measured changes centered on per-line output formatting and were slower than that candidate. Because the measurements combine or alter multiple aspects, they do not establish the isolated benefit of any one transformation. The four-core target makes parallel work possible, but the observed queue-based attempt regressed substantially; integration must be measured, not inferred.\"},\"error\":null,\"infrastructure_error\":false,\"model\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"nonce\":\"4ca249002aa0420fb096ec877d35856a\",\"protocol\":\"hpc-forest-codex-mailbox-v1\",\"reasoning_effort\":\"max\",\"request_sha256\":\"d5bbce62e20ba0fc8b0adec2e99223307782254861bc669c9dea2d3fe2df2910\",\"session_id\":\"c08531b8446a447bacb06c4500c0c033\",\"usage\":{\"cached_tokens_included_in_input\":true,\"codex_jsonl_sha256\":\"63b4b2a2cba5323fba5225f9a10fe60baa41d864244c7ae47607c05702e857e7\",\"events\":[{\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"cached_input_tokens\":0,\"input_tokens\":18883,\"output_tokens\":4491,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":3911}],\"llm_calls\":1,\"model_requested\":\"gpt-6-luna\",\"reasoning_effort_requested\":\"max\",\"token_accounting\":\"exact\",\"tokens\":23374,\"tool_events\":[]}},\"status\":\"complete\"}",
      "created": 1792124979.5400133
